<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilbour Papyrus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1145 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wilbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> is the largest known non-funerary papyrus from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, an administrative survey of cultivatable land made in the 4th year of Ramesses V, around 1145 BCE, in the late Ramesside Period. Ten metres long, it covers about 140 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> to near Tihna: Text A lists the holders of plots, among them priests, soldiers, ladies, herdsmen, stable-masters and farmers, and Text B the crown lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. It suggests that temples held 13 to 18 percent of Egypt's farmland, though whether the grain it records was tax or rent is unknown. Charles Edwin Wilbour bought it at Elephantine in 1893; it is in the Brooklyn Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilbour-papyrus.jpg" alt="Wilbour Papyrus" width="330" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by unknown person in the Brooklyn Museum Ancient Egypt collection Original author was in Ancient Egypt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilbur_papyrus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Text A, the longer part, is a ledger of the names and occupations of those holding plots of land, in four sections of which the first is lost. Its second section opens with a date in the fourth year of Ramesses V, in the Inundation season, which allows the document to be dated. Within the region surveyed it covers only about 4,630 of the roughly 150,000 hectares that would have been arable. Text B is shorter but was written earlier; it records the khato-land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and, unlike Text A, gives the grain yields and where they were collected.</p><p>Text A sorts plots into four kinds: ihwty, small plots held by individual field labourers or tenant farmers; m-drt, held collectively by several such people; rwdw, held by an administrator such as a priest; and rmnyt, held by institutions such as temples. The small plots had poorer soil and were expected to yield about 100 litres of grain per hectare, the larger ones up to 3,000. The holders include many foreigners, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> and Near Easterners, possibly descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> settled on land given for military service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> records grain passing between farmers and the state, but whether this was tax, rent or both is unknown. If the ihwty farmers owned their plots, the grain was a tax at a rate far lower than expected, perhaps because they also owed goods such as pottery and textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour in the off season; if they rented from the state, it was rent. The larger plots handed over 30 percent of their harvest. The survey may have been made to review these rates, and was likely ordered by the Chief Taxing Master, the official in charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s finances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> may be an archival copy rather than the original survey, and most of its first section decayed before it was found. Charles Edwin Wilbour bought it among seventeen papyri from a farmer on Elephantine, near Aswan. After his death in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> hotel in 1896 his belongings were kept in storage there, and when they were returned to his family nearly half a century later his widow gave the papyri to the Brooklyn Museum. Alan Gardiner translated the document for the museum in 1941, and Egyptologists have since used it to study how the Egyptian state worked.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilbour Papyrus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings gave them fields, captives and the spoils of war, above all to Amun of Karnak, and modern reckonings from the Harris Papyrus and the <b>Wilbour Papyrus</b> put roughly 13–18 per cent of Egypt’s farmland in their hands under the Ramesside kings, most of it Amun’s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
